+## 14. Validate portability within the supported target matrix
+
+Vendor-specific instructions (for example TMA/WGMMA on NVIDIA or MFMA on AMD)
+require compatible device and SDK capabilities. Generic primitives such as
+`thread_idx`, `barrier`, `warp.sum`, shared memory and TileTensor are a reason to
+investigate portability, not proof that a kernel works on every vendor or device.
+
+- Establish the project's supported devices, architectures, SDK and runtime first.
+  Keep justified capability guards until equivalent target evidence supports a
+  change. Do not expand the supported matrix merely because an example is generic.
+- Where portability is in scope, run correctness and boundary tests on the required
+  targets or their configured CI lanes. Missing hardware is a capability gap;
+  a run on one device does not establish acceptance on another.
+- Keep a vendor-specific fast path small and retain an appropriate generic fallback
+  when the project contract requires one. Match capability checks to the selected
+  SDK's host/device distinction.
+- Size vectorization, unrolling and tiles from real device limits. Apply an
+  optimization only after correctness and equivalent synchronized benchmarks on
+  the selected workloads; generic syntax does not imply universal speedups.
+- For a necessary target restriction, record the actual missing capability or
+  numerical failure, owner and review condition using project conventions.
+  Creating an external issue is a separate action, not a prerequisite imposed here.
+- Query hardware properties rather than hard-code one vendor's warp or shared
+  memory size; see section 13.
 
 ## 15. Hygiene
 
-- Every temporary hack or API abuse gets a `TODO(<ticket>)` linking a
-  filed issue.
+- Record temporary workarounds with their reason, owner and review condition
+  using the project's existing tracking convention. Do not create external tickets
+  solely because this guide was loaded.
 - Keep comments short: a line or two on *why*, never a paragraph. Don't
   explain what the code plainly shows; delete comments that add nothing
   (`# (TileTensor-based)`).
